 lists Ravi Menon first for version7.go, with Mei Chen and MikeWang after. It gives no line shares and does not credit Mei with the Time7 accessor. Ravi is presented as the main person for the file.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 3, \"reason\": \"Answer A says the SQL history is in sql_test.go and admits it did not follow the rename. It names Ravi Menon as the SQL person and gives no 44% figure for Overfelt, so it never traces the history through the rename.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 4, \"reason\": \"Answer A says Ravi is the only active person but also lists Noor Haddad and Kofi Mensah as having recent commits. It does not mention Alex Rowe as active. It gives no repo-wide share of lines owned by departed people.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 5, \"reason\": \"Answer A names Sam Okafor as a reasonable second reviewer for time.go and lists Noor Haddad as recently active. Both are noise that must not be named as owners or experts.\", \"pass\": false}], \"B\": [{\"n\": 1, \"reason\": \"Answer B says Sam Okafor's 100% blame on time.go is only a line-ending commit, which is correct. But it credits MikeWang, Youngjae Lee, Nicu Micle and Rowe as authors of time.go and never names Paul Borman or his 73% share.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 2, \"reason\": \"Answer B says MikeWang wrote 86% of version7.go (truth 81%, range 72-90), and Mei Chen wrote 9%. Ravi Menon is listed only as one of the contributors of a few lines. It does not say Ravi committed Mei's Time7 patch, but it does not credit Ravi with her lines either.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 3, \"reason\": \"Answer B follows the rename from sql.go to database_sql.go and names Justin Overfelt at 44%. It also names Andrew Stone, Daniel Souza, David E. Wheeler and Paul Borman, and says none are active. It correctly attributes the rename to Ravi without making Ravi the author.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 4, \"reason\": \"Answer B says 37 of 40 files are mostly owned by authors inactive over a year, which is a file count and not a share of lines. It names Ravi and Rowe as active but hedges that Rowe is split across two names, though it also suggests they are the same person. It does not mention Kofi Mensah as active. It lacks the 93-98% line share and the per-file active shares.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 5, \"reason\": \"Answer B explains that Sam Okafor's time.go blame comes from a line-ending commit and that Sam owns no real code. It treats the 1970 Kofi date as bad metadata, which is correct. It does not name Noor Haddad as an owner.\", \"pass\": true}], \"C\": [{\"n\": 1, \"reason\": \"Answer C names Paul Borman as original author with 6 of 13 commits and says his last time.go commit was 2016. It gives no line share and a wrong last-commit date (truth 2023). It also lists Sam Okafor as the most recent changer, so it does not credit him with owning the code, but the main-author claim is weak.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 2, \"reason\": \"Answer C names MikeWang among the original version7.go authors with no line share. It mentions Mei Chen only as the latest change before Ravi's. It does not credit Mei with the Time7 accessor or give a share.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 3, \"reason\": \"Answer C says it did not trace the history across the rename and gives no line shares. It does not name Overfelt as the largest author.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 4, \"reason\": \"Answer C names Ravi and Rowe as active and notes that Rowe appears under 'arowe' and 'Alex Rowe'. It names Sam Okafor and Mei Chen as reviewers and gives no repo-wide share of lines.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 5, \"reason\": \"Answer C lists Sam Okafor as someone to ask, since he made the most recent time.go change. That treats him as an expert, which the criterion forbids.\", \"pass\": false}], \"D\": [{\"n\": 1, \"reason\": \"Answer D names Paul Borman as the time.go main author by commit count (6 commits, last 2016) with no line share. Its 2016 date is wrong (truth 2023). It does say he is no longer active.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 2, \"reason\": \"Answer D lists MikeWang with 2 commits, Ravi and Mei Chen, with no line shares. It does not credit Mei with the Time7 accessor, and it suggests Ravi as the reviewer.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 3, \"reason\": \"Answer D gives SQL authors by commit count (Wheeler, Overfelt, Stone, Souza). It does not follow the rename or name Overfelt at 44%, and it makes Ravi the sole reviewer.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 4, \"reason\": \"Answer D recommends Ravi and Alex Rowe. It hedges that Alex Rowe may be outside the 12-month window, gives no repo-wide share of lines, and does not mention Kofi.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 5, \"reason\": \"Answer D calls Sam Okafor a one-off contributor and asks the user to check whether he is around, which treats him as a possible expert. It does not discuss the bots or Kofi.\", \"pass\": false}], \"E\": [{\"n\": 1, \"reason\": \"Answer E uses git blame and names Paul Borman with 111 of 152 lines (73%) on time.go. It says he is probably gone but dates his last commit to about 2021 (truth 2023) and hedges on whether later 'bormanp' commits are the same person. It lists Sam Okafor only as a recent fallback and does not credit him with the lines. The core claim is mostly right, but the hedge on Borman's identity and the wrong date weaken it.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 2, \"reason\": \"Answer E gives MikeWang 101 of 117 lines (86%, within the 72-90% range) and Mei Chen 11 lines (about 9%). Ravi Menon gets 2 lines and is not credited with Mei's lines. It does not say that Ravi committed Mei's patch.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 3, \"reason\": \"Answer E follows the rename to database_sql.go with git blame. It gives Justin Overfelt 26 lines, Stone 11, Wheeler 8 and Souza 8, and says all are gone. It says Ravi's only commit was the rename.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 4, \"reason\": \"Answer E says 'nobody is active by the 90-day test' and recommends Ravi, Rowe, Mei Chen and Okafor as reviewers. It gives no repo-wide 95.9% share, does not mention Kofi as active, and recommends Mei Chen and Okafor, who are not active.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 5, \"reason\": \"Answer E lists Sam Okafor as a fallback reviewer for time.go and recommends him in the 'Who to ask' list. That treats him as an expert, and it treats Kofi's date as a quirk rather than as an active person.\", \"pass\": false}], \"F\": [{\"n\": 1, \"reason\": \"Answer F gives Paul Borman 111 of 152 lines (73.0%) in time.go and says he is inactive, with the Okafor line-ending commit giving no lines. It dates his last commit to the file to 2016 and says he has had no commit anywhere since 2016 (truth 2023). It also says the tool found nothing to merge for him but notes his four addresses as unconfirmed duplicates. It gets the share and the inactive status right but is wrong on the date.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 2, \"reason\": \"Answer F gives MikeWang 81.2% of version7.go. It credits Mei Chen with Time7 (11 lines, 9.4%) and says Ravi Menon committed it and holds only 2 lines (1.9%). It recommends asking Mei first, but the attribution is correct.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 3, \"reason\": \"Answer F follows database_sql.go back to sql.go with git blame -M -C. It names Justin Overfelt at 44.1% and also lists Stone, Wheeler, Souza and Borman, and says all are inactive. Ravi is not credited as the author.\", \"pass\": true}, {\"n\": 4, \"reason\": \"Answer F says 95.7% of non-blank lines belong to people with no recent commit, which is in the 93-98% range. It says Alex Rowe is the active bus-factor person and that SQL has no active authors. It lists 4 people active in the last year (not the 3 in the truth) and does not name them all. It treats Mei Chen as active and recommends her for version7.go. That conflicts with the truth that none of the three files has an active author with a meaningful share, and the criterion requires the active set to be exactly Ravi, Rowe and Kofi.\", \"pass\": false}, {\"n\": 5, \"reason\": \"Answer F says Okafor's line-ending commit gives him no lines and does not name Noor Haddad as an owner. It does not treat bots as people. It never mentions Kofi Mensah's date and reports 4 active people, which suggests it may count Mei Chen rather than Kofi. The Kofi handling cannot be confirmed as correct.\", \"pass\": false}]}","ttft_ms":22091,"type":"result","duration_ms":22114,"uuid":"09e6f42d-9c63-453b-9eaf-c156082af820","ttft_stream_ms":1976,"time_to_request_ms":418,"first_content_frame_ms":1976,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
